# {{ project_name }}

Generated from the Paved CDK template (SDK variant).

## Defining your service

You write plain decorated functions in `handlers/*.py`; `app.py` only calls `synth()`.

```python
# handlers/example.py
from paved_cdk.sdk import data_api, function

data_api("{{ project_slug }}-data")   # S3 bucket + private API Gateway

@function()                            # in-VPC, KMS-encrypted Lambda
def predict(event, context):
    return {"statusCode": 200}
```

Add more functions (`@function`, `@scheduled("rate(1 day)")`, `@api.post("/x")`) as
new modules under `handlers/`. The platform discovers them at synth time.

## First time

```bash
uv sync          # pulls the platform library straight from GitHub (pinned tag)
uv run python app.py   # synth - deterministic, no AWS login, no Node
```

The platform library is declared as a git dependency in `pyproject.toml` (no extra
package registry needed). `uv sync` clones it at tag `{{ platform_version }}`.

## Deployment - three stages, one pipeline

The **central, shared pipeline** (maintained in `{{ platform_repo }}`) decides what
each trigger does:

- **push to `main`** -> deploy to **dev**
- **open/update a PR** -> ephemeral preview stack in dev (destroyed when the PR closes)
- **push a `vX.Y.Z` tag** -> release: promote **dev -> preprod -> prod**

Every DS project runs the exact same pipeline; it updates when you `copier update`
(which bumps the `@{{ platform_version }}` ref in `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`).

You configure **nothing** AWS-specific per repo: the platform owns all deploy auth
(OIDC roles and account ids) centrally. Your team's three accounts are provisioned
for you.

## Pulling platform updates

```bash
copier update     # re-renders against the latest template, bumps the platform tag
```

## What you do NOT configure

VPC, subnets, KMS encryption, IAM permissions boundary, log retention and
governance tags are resolved automatically from whichever of your three accounts
the pipeline is deploying into. Just add decorated handlers under `handlers/`.
